<h2>Why a separate application</h2>
<p>My earlier ERPNext work focused on Fabric Store stock, batch and gate-pass workflows. I started REVE ONE to bring related garment-manufacturing tasks into a focused application interface, while retaining ERPNext as the transactional foundation.</p>
<p>I started REVE ONE to build that application layer: an operator should be able to find a factory order, understand its next step and follow related materials or quality work without navigating unrelated ERP screens. It is distinct from the <a href="/work/erp-system">legacy ERPNext customization</a>.</p>

<h2>Architecture and ownership</h2>
<p>A Vue 3 and TypeScript interface runs on a Frappe application backed by Python APIs and ERPNext. Vue Router separates the workspaces; Tailwind and Frappe UI support a consistent shell and reusable record interfaces. Frappe sessions, CSRF checks and server permissions remain the authority for access.</p>
