<p>ERPNext owns native stock and accounting records. REVE ONE adds garment-specific interfaces and related workflow context. Keeping those responsibilities distinct avoids maintaining a competing inventory ledger.</p>

<h2>Interface engineering</h2>
<p>The application shell provides grouped navigation, dark and light themes, session-aware controls, search and notifications. Record pages share headers, list controls and clear loading, empty, error and permission states. The interface is designed around the next action and the relevant evidence, rather than a dashboard full of disconnected totals.</p>
<p>Order 360 gathers related activity and operational context in one place. Navigation visibility follows role capabilities, but hiding a button is never treated as a security boundary: the backend checks the action too.</p>

<h2>Application workspaces</h2>
<p><strong>Factory orders, planning and production.</strong> Factory orders, planning and production are connected workspaces. The interface brings related order context and production progress together so users can follow the work across stages.</p>
<p><strong>Materials and store operations.</strong> Material requirements, stock visibility, batches, physical rolls, scanning, issues, transfers and returns are organized as operator tasks. Stock postings use native ERPNext transactions.</p>
<p><strong>Cutting consumption and remnants.</strong> Cutting interfaces connect issued fabric with returned remnants and related order context. The aim is to make consumption understandable while keeping the supporting stock transactions traceable.</p>
<p><strong>Quality and corrective actions.</strong> Checks, defects and follow-up actions have explicit ownership and state. They connect to Order 360 and related production context so an unresolved quality issue can be followed through the workflow.</p>
<p><strong>Shared workflows.</strong> Activity, notifications and search help users find related records. Workforce, packing and reporting workspaces extend the same application structure. Implemented modules are not a claim that every department has adopted them.</p>

<h2>Gate passes, rolls and inventory review</h2>
<p>Gate-pass and physical-roll interfaces connect material handling with inventory context. Stocktake and reconciliation work provide a separate place to review counted stock and differences. These are software capabilities and development areas; their inclusion here does not imply accepted physical balances or production adoption.</p>
